WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.890 --> 00:00:05.339
There have been great horror movies from every
decade, but if you get brutal and start hacking

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:09.900
away, eventually each decade will have just
one classic horror film left standing.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:14.800
Directed by Jennifer Kent, The Babadook deals
with one of the most serious and studied topics

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:16.540
in all of horror: grief.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:22.060
Whether the title's top-hat wearing demon
is real, metaphorical, or a weird combo of

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:25.900
both, he's one of the most powerful symbols
of death and anguish in cinema history.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:30.349
Then there's Essie Davis, whose performance
as a mother on the verge of collapse is both

00:00:30.349 --> 00:00:31.780
heartbreaking and horrific.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:36.120
And even looking past the symbolism, The Babadook
is just straight-up terrifying.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:41.320
Everything here drips with dread, from that
creepy pop-up book to Noah Wiseman's devilish

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.100
performance as a troubled little kid.

00:00:43.449 --> 00:00:47.940
In a decade filled with remarkable films,
The Babadook that will haunt horror fans for

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:48.920
years to come.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:51.280
"See him in your room at night…"

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.520
"Mom, does it hurt the boy?

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:56.980
Mom!"

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:02.839
Set in a desolate Stockholm, Let the Right
One In follows a 12-year-old boy named Oskar

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.200
who befriends a seemingly young girl named
Eli.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:08.700
Both look innocent, but they harbor disturbing
secrets.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.400
Oskar is regularly bullied at school and spends
his nights plotting gruesome revenge.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:18.680
And Eli is a centuries-old vampire who drinks
the life force of human beings.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:22.460
Bleak and snowy on the surface, the movie's
heart is bloody and red.

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:26.600
It isn't easy being a monster, and Let the
Right One In takes a look at these twisted

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:29.360
creatures in all their messy, murderous complexity.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:34.659
The Silence of the Lambs is the only horror
movie so far to win the Best Picture Oscar,

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:37.100
and only a fool would argue that it wasn't
deserved.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:42.159
While the movie is anchored by Jodie Foster's
tough-as-nails performance as Clarice Starling,

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:45.440
its legend has lasted so long thanks to Anthony
Hopkins.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:50.100
As Hannibal Lecter, Hopkins created one of
the greatest movie monsters of all time.

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:55.600
Calm, creepy, and charismatic, he can discuss
literature one minute, devour your liver the

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:56.100
next.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:59.430
"A census taker once tried to test me.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:05.180
I ate his liver with some fava beans a nice
chianti."

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:11.040
Directed by the legendary Stanley Kubrick,
The Shining is a big, cold, labyrinth of a

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:11.540
movie.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:15.540
Kubrick's use of the steadicam — following
little Danny Torrance around the Overlook

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:19.930
— is groundbreaking, and both Jack Nicholson
and Shelley Duvall give heightened performances

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:22.860
that perfectly match the overwhelming presence
of the hotel.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:27.520
While it's a technical masterpiece, it's also
one of the scariest films ever made.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.360
The Shining is a nightmare that never stops,
and just like a bad dream, we'll be trying

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.600
to decipher its meaning for years to come.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.320
"Stay away from me."

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:37.280
"Wendy.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:38.780
Darling.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:40.620
Light of my life.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:42.650
I'm not gonna hurt you.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:44.260
You didn't let me finish my sentence."

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:49.370
William Friedkin's The Exorcist is a film
so iconic that it's become the standard for

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:51.280
every scary movie ever since.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:56.680
Nearly 50 years later, we've seen this possession
story a million times, so it might be hard

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:02.159
for modern audiences to appreciate The Exorcist
as the world-shattering, nausea-inducing game-changer

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:02.760
that it was.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:07.239
At the time, the special effects were out
of this world, and audiences were repelled

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:08.860
by the puking and the head-twisting.

00:03:09.409 --> 00:03:14.220
But under all the slimy green goo, this movie
works so well because it's got such beautifully

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:15.600
well-written characters.

00:03:15.769 --> 00:03:19.960
The acting is Oscar-worthy, and the final
confrontation between good and evil still

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:20.460
resonates.

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:25.860
Directed by Alfred Hitchcock, Psycho was a
game-changing film that's buried its way deep

00:03:25.879 --> 00:03:27.830
into the pop culture consciousness.

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:32.220
Bernard Herrmann's violent score is just as
iconic as Anthony Perkins' performance as

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:34.280
the quietly creepy Norman Bates.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:38.600
He's both sympathetic and psychotic, and that
infamous shower scene is still one of the

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:40.440
scariest horror moments of all time.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:48.820
“ahh”

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:53.840
Sure, it's been parodied to death, but in
1960, it was an absolute shock.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:58.480
With that brutal knife attack, Psycho became
the granddaddy of the slasher genre.

00:03:58.489 --> 00:04:02.610
While it's not as bloody as modern horror,
Psycho is still an unsettling film that will

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:04.860
make you think twice about checking into a
hotel.

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:10.209
In Invasion of the Body Snatchers, Dr. Miles
Bennell is a clean-cut doctor living in a

00:04:10.209 --> 00:04:11.849
wholesome American neighborhood.

00:04:11.849 --> 00:04:16.600
However, the good doctor soon realizes that
all his friends are being replaced by alien

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:17.500
doppelgängers.

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:22.100
These replacements are cold, calculating,
and planning to take over the world, using

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:25.100
plant pods that grow clones to replace human
beings.

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:29.660
Sure, the idea of pod people might sound a
little corny, and some of the effects are

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:34.310
a bit hokey, but the movie is still effective
because it's completely soaked in suspicion

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:34.900
and dread.

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:39.890
The idea of being replaced — consumed and
changed by some malevolent force — is truly

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:40.520
chilling.

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:45.700
Cat People follows a young woman named Irene
who's got a bit of trouble in the intimacy

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:46.260
department.

00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:50.020
She's worried about getting intimate with
her new husband because she thinks if she

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:53.700
gets aroused, she'll turn into a black panther
and rip his head off.

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:58.520
It's a pretty strong case for abstinence,
although we're not sure if Irene is just troubled

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:01.720
or if there's actually some evil magic running
through her family tree.

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:04.460
And that's why Cat People is such a masterpiece.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:08.840
It plays on the fear of the unknown by keeping
the killer cat-woman offscreen.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:13.800
In the film's most famous sequence, a woman
is walking down a deserted street late at

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:17.840
night, becoming more and more sure that she's
being pursued by something inhuman.

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:22.000
It's a powerfully paced sequence and proof
enough that Cat People holds up.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:27.920
Released in 1931, Dracula wasn't the first
vampire movie, or even the first movie inspired

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:29.420
by Bram Stoker's novel.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:34.060
But it did establish the undead ideal, all
thanks to actor Bela Lugosi.

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:39.280
With his hypnotic hands and Hungarian accent,
Lugosi set the mold for every vampire film

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:44.640
to come, and every line he delivers is both
creepy and charming, aristocratic and eerie.

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:49.420
Thanks to this seductive bloodsucker, horror
became one of Hollywood's most popular genres.

